---
import Styleguide from '../../layouts/Styleguide.astro';
import { colorTokens, typeSteps, typeTokens, spaceTokens, gridTokens } from '../../styleguide/tokens.mjs';
import { BREAKPOINTS, COLUMN_BREAKPOINTS, TYPE_SCALE } from '../../styleguide/components.mjs';

const tokens = colorTokens(process.cwd());
// The scale's sizes come from site-shell.css; what each step is for comes from the registry.
const steps = typeSteps(process.cwd()).map((t) => ({ ...t, ...TYPE_SCALE.find((x) => x.step === t.step) }));
const LAYER_ROWS = [
  ['base', 'The reset, every token and the element defaults, all in site-shell.css.'],
  ['site', 'The shell every page shares (header, footer, button, label) and the article template, article.css.'],
  ['kit', 'The article kit, article-kit.css.'],
  ['page', 'One page\'s own sheet: the home page, a service page, the front door, /ui.'],
  ['utilities', 'Single-purpose classes that always win: .accent and .sr-only.'],
];
const base = typeTokens(process.cwd());
const space = spaceTokens(process.cwd());
const grid = gridTokens(process.cwd());
const GRID_USE = {
  '--container-page': 'The page: header, footer, service pages',
  '--container-article': 'An article, and the width a calculator or pinned sequence widens to',
  '--gutter': 'Inside each container; 20px below 760',
  '--rail': 'The source-notes rail, 1180 and up',
  '--rail-gap': 'Between the reading column and the rail',
  '--measure--4': 'Pull quotes, short headings',
  '--measure--3': 'Section headings, legends, captions beside a number',
  '--measure--2': 'Short notes',
  '--measure--1': 'Decks, pinned steps, record text',
  '--measure-0': 'The reading column',
  '--measure-1': 'Wide text: tables of notes, the styleguide',
};
// What each base token is for; the values come from site-shell.css.
const USE = {
  '--font-display': 'Archivo Black: titles, section headings, big numbers',
  '--font-serif': 'Source Serif 4: reading text, decks, pull quotes',
  '--font-sans': 'IBM Plex Sans: everything else, and the body default',
  '--font-mono': 'Code in the styleguide',
  '--weight-regular': 'Text',
  '--weight-semibold': 'Labels, notes that need weight',
  '--weight-bold': 'Headings, names, emphasis; the default for strong and headings',
  '--leading-display': 'Capitals in the display face, big numbers',
  '--leading-heading': 'Headings and titles in mixed case',
  '--leading-snug': 'Labels, controls, subheads, short lines',
  '--leading-text': 'Small text: captions, notes, sources',
  '--leading-body': 'Reading text; the body default',
  '--leading-cap': 'The drop cap\'s box, so it sits on the lines beside it',
  '--tracking-caps': 'Small capitals; everything else is 0',
};
const minus = (n) => (n < 0 ? `\u2212${Math.abs(n)}` : String(n));
// Where the reading sizes sit at each width.
const ROLES = [
  ['Body text', 'Source Serif 4, old-style numerals', '17', '20', '20'],
  ['Deck', 'Source Serif 4 italic', '17', '20', '20'],
  ['Lede and subheads', 'Source Serif 4; the lede opens on an 86px drop cap', '20', '24', '24'],
  ['Pull quote', 'Source Serif 4 italic, the mark hung in the margin', '20', '24', '29'],
  ['Section heading', 'Archivo Black, numbered in Source Serif', '24', '24', '29'],
  ['Article title', 'Archivo Black, capitals', '35', '41', '50, then 60 from 1180'],
  ['Label and caption', 'IBM Plex Sans', '14', '14', '14'],
];
// What the article itself does at each width, for the table under the scale.
const ARTICLE = [
  ['1180 and up', 'Sources sit in the rail beside the text. A pinned sequence takes the column and the rail together.'],
  ['Below 1180', 'Sources open under the paragraph that cites them. A pinned graphic becomes a card at the top of the screen with the steps under it.'],
  ['1040 and up', 'A calculator widens past the column to the article container, and its main fields sit in one row.'],
  ['Below 760', 'Body text drops a step to 17px, and the lede, subheads and pull quotes to 20px. The pull quote mark moves above the quote.'],
  ['Column under 560', 'Kit components take their compact form: bar labels without their words, stacked answers, smaller numbers.'],
  ['Calculator under 900', 'Its fields sit two across under 560 and three across from 560.'],
];
---
<Styleguide title="Foundations" description="Colour tokens, type, rules, layout, accessibility and performance for articles on pauldrago.com." path="/ui/foundations">
  <h1 class="display-title ui-title">Foundations</h1>
  <p class="ui-lede">A pale cool ground, navy ink and one terracotta accent; three typefaces; hard rules; one easing for every movement. Both themes share one set of token names, and dark mode follows the reader's browser.</p>

  <section class="ui-section" id="colour">
    <h2 class="ui-h2">Colour</h2>
    <p class="ui-p">Read from <code>public/assets/site-shell.css</code> when the site builds, where each colour is written once as <code>light-dark(light, dark)</code>. Each chip shows the light value over the dark one. Contrast is measured against the page ground; body text needs 4.5 to 1.</p>
    <div class="ui-swatches">
      {tokens.map((t) => (
        <div class="ui-swatch">
          <div class="ui-swatch-chips" aria-hidden="true"><span style={`background:${t.light}`}></span><span style={`background:${t.dark}`}></span></div>
          <div>
            <b>{t.name}</b>
            <p>{t.role}</p>
            <p><span class="ui-num">{t.light}</span> light, <span class="ui-num">{t.dark}</span> dark</p>
            {t.text && <p>Contrast <span class="ui-num">{t.lightContrast}:1</span> light, <span class="ui-num">{t.darkContrast}:1</span> dark</p>}
            {t.onBrass && <p>On the accent <span class="ui-num">{t.onBrass[0]}:1</span> light, <span class="ui-num">{t.onBrass[1]}:1</span> dark</p>}
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <ul class="ui-list">
      <li>The accent marks what the sentence is about: one bar, one number, the section numbers. Two highlights in a figure only when the sentence names two.</li>
      <li>Context bars are the secondary colour mixed toward the ground, so they sit below the accent in both themes.</li>
      <li>Colour never carries a reading alone. Values are printed, hollow and hatched bars carry their own meaning, and forced-colors mode draws bars in the text colour.</li>
      <li>Illustrations have their own palette: terracotta #D9552B on the light ground and brass #E0A938 on navy.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="ui-section" id="type">
    <h2 class="ui-h2">Type</h2>
    <p class="ui-p">Archivo Black for display, Source Serif 4 for reading, IBM Plex Sans for figures, labels and controls. All three are self-hosted, and each has a fallback sized to its metrics so nothing shifts when the font arrives.</p>
    <h3 class="ui-h3">One root</h3>
    <p class="ui-p">The stylesheets sit in five cascade layers, declared once at the top of <code>site-shell.css</code>. A later layer wins over an earlier one whatever its selectors, so a component never needs <code>!important</code> to beat the base, and the order the sheets load in stops mattering. The linter keeps every rule in one of them (<code>css-layer</code>).</p>
    <div class="ui-table-wrap">
      <table class="ui-table">
        <thead><tr><th class="label">Layer</th><th class="label">What sits in it</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{LAYER_ROWS.map(([l, what]) => <tr><td><code>{l}</code></td><td>{what}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="ui-p"><code>site-shell.css</code> loads first on every page and holds the basics: the colours, the three typefaces, the type scale, three weights, five kinds of line spacing and the tracking for small capitals. It also sets the defaults every element inherits, so an element nobody styled still lands on the scale. Every other sheet uses these tokens and sets only what differs from what it inherits. The linter rejects a size, face, weight, line spacing or tracking that is not a token (<code>type-scale</code>, <code>type-tokens</code>).</p>
    <div class="ui-table-wrap">
      <table class="ui-table">
        <thead><tr><th class="label">Token</th><th class="label">Value</th><th class="label">Used for</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{[...base.fonts, ...base.weights, ...base.leading, ...base.tracking].map((t) => <tr><td><code>var({t.name})</code></td><td class="ui-num">{t.name.startsWith('--font-') ? t.value.split(',')[0].replace(/'/g, '') : t.value}</td><td>{USE[t.name] ?? ''}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <h3 class="ui-h3">The scale</h3>
    <p class="ui-p">Every font size on the site is one of these thirteen steps, read here from <code>site-shell.css</code>. Body text is step 0, each step is 1.2 times the one below it, and 14px is the smallest text anywhere. The linter rejects any other size (<code>type-scale</code>). See it set on the <a href="/ui/components/type-scale">type scale</a> page.</p>
    <div class="ui-table-wrap">
      <table class="ui-table">
        <thead><tr><th class="label">Step</th><th class="label">Token</th><th class="label">Size</th><th class="label">Used for</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{steps.map((t) => <tr><td class="ui-num">{minus(t.step)}</td><td><code>var({t.name})</code></td><td class="ui-num">{t.px}px</td><td>{t.roles}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <h3 class="ui-h3">Reading sizes at each width</h3>
    <div class="ui-table-wrap">
      <table class="ui-table">
        <thead><tr><th class="label">Role</th><th class="label">Face</th><th class="label">Phone</th><th class="label">760 to 1040</th><th class="label">From 1040</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{ROLES.map(([role, face, a, b, c]) => <tr><td>{role}</td><td>{face}</td><td class="ui-num">{a}</td><td class="ui-num">{b}</td><td class="ui-num">{c}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <ul class="ui-list">
      <li>A size changes only at a breakpoint, so every size is a step at every width.</li>
      <li>Two things the reader has to tell apart sit at least a step apart, or differ in face or weight.</li>
      <li>Small capitals are the <a href="/ui/components/label">label</a>, and numbers in the display face are the <a href="/ui/components/number">number</a>, in four sizes. Neither is restyled by the components that use them.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="ui-section" id="spacing">
    <h2 class="ui-h2">Spacing</h2>
    <p class="ui-p">Every margin, padding and gap on the site is a step of one scale, read here from <code>site-shell.css</code>. It sits on a 4px grid, each token is named for how many 4px units it holds, and the steps widen as they grow. The linter rejects any other value (<code>space-tokens</code>).</p>
    <div class="ui-space">
      {space.map((t) => <div class="ui-space-row"><code>var({t.name})</code><span class="ui-num">{t.value}</span><i style={`width:${t.value}`}></i></div>)}
    </div>
    <ul class="ui-list">
      <li>Sections open var(--space-20) below the last paragraph, var(--space-12) on phones, with a hairline and the section's numeral.</li>
      <li>Figures sit var(--space-12) from the text around them.</li>
      <li>Two things that belong together sit a small step apart; two groups sit at least two steps apart.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="ui-section" id="rules">
    <h2 class="ui-h2">Rules</h2>
    <p class="ui-p">A few weights of rule carry the structure of every page.</p>
    <div class="ui-rules-demo">
      <div><i style="border-top:1px solid var(--hairline)"></i><span>1px hairline: over every section, above and below figures, between ledger rows.</span></div>
      <div><i style="border-top:2px solid var(--brass)"></i><span>2px accent: over a unit stat, under a pull quote.</span></div>
      <div><i style="border-top:2px solid var(--paper)"></i><span>2px ink: over a ledger's total and the author box.</span></div>
      <div><i style="border-top:4px solid var(--brass)"></i><span>4px accent: over the short version.</span></div>
    </div>
    <ul class="ui-list">
      <li>Corners are square, inputs included.</li>
      <li>No gradients. The one shadow is under the graphic pinned at the top of a phone screen, so the steps scroll under it.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="ui-section" id="layout">
    <h2 class="ui-h2">Layout grid</h2>
    <p class="ui-p">Every page changes layout at four widths and nowhere else, and the linter holds every stylesheet to them (<code>css-breakpoint</code>). Components do not measure the screen: they answer to the column they sit in, so a figure reads the same in an article, a /ui frame or a sidebar. See it move on the <a href="/ui/components/layout-grid">layout grid</a> page.</p>
    <h3 class="ui-h3">The page</h3>
    <div class="ui-table-wrap">
      <table class="ui-table">
        <thead><tr><th class="label">Width</th><th class="label">Name</th><th class="label">What changes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{BREAKPOINTS.map((b) => <tr><td class="ui-num">{b.px}px</td><td>{b.name}</td><td>{b.changes}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <h3 class="ui-h3">The column</h3>
    <div class="ui-table-wrap">
      <table class="ui-table">
        <thead><tr><th class="label">Width</th><th class="label">Name</th><th class="label">What changes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{COLUMN_BREAKPOINTS.map((b) => <tr><td class="ui-num">{b.px}px</td><td>{b.name}</td><td>{b.changes}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <h3 class="ui-h3">Grid tokens</h3>
    <p class="ui-p">Read from <code>site-shell.css</code>. Media queries cannot read custom properties, so the breakpoints above stay numbers and the linter holds them; everything else about the grid is a token. Measures step by 1.2 from the reading measure, like the type scale.</p>
    <div class="ui-table-wrap">
      <table class="ui-table">
        <thead><tr><th class="label">Token</th><th class="label">Value</th><th class="label">Used for</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{grid.map((t) => <tr><td><code>var({t.name})</code></td><td class="ui-num">{t.value.startsWith('var(') ? t.value.replace(/var\((--[\w-]+)\)/, (m, n) => (space.find((x) => x.name === n)?.value ?? m)) : t.value}</td><td>{GRID_USE[t.name] ?? ''}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <ul class="ui-list">
      <li>The gutter is a step narrower below 760: 20px. Everything that reads it follows, including the pinned card and the opener that run to the page edge.</li>
      <li>A calculator, 1040 and up, and a pinned sequence, 1180 and up, widen to the article container, less its gutters.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 class="ui-h3">Choosing the step</h3>
    <ul class="ui-list">
      <li>Pick the lowest step where every column still reads. Two columns sit side by side from 760, and so do three or four short items. Three columns led by a big headline stack until 1040.</li>
      <li>A tool keeps its controls beside its result from 760. A table keeps its columns from 760; below it a site ledger becomes labeled cards.</li>
      <li>Ease the tablet band. Inside (760px &lt;= width &lt; 1040px) gaps and type take a smaller step of their scale, and only a fixed track width may use clamp().</li>
      <li>A rule scoped to a range has to help at both ends of it.</li>
      <li>Check each change before and after at 759 and 760, 1039 and 1040, and at 768, 820 and 960. Turn a phone sideways (844 by 390) for anything sticky or set by a script.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3 class="ui-h3">How to write it</h3>
    <pre class="ui-terminal"><code>{`/* The page: a width from the scale, in range syntax */
@media (width < 760px) { ... }
@media (760px <= width < 1180px) { ... }

/* The tablet band: a smaller step of the same scale */
.closing-band-grid { gap: var(--space-16); }
@media (760px <= width < 1040px) {
  .closing-band-grid { gap: var(--space-12); }
}

/* A component: the column it sits in */
@container column (width < 560px) { ... }

/* A script: the same query, and it listens for changes */
const tablet = matchMedia('(width >= 760px)');
tablet.addEventListener('change', update);`}</code></pre>
    <h3 class="ui-h3">What an article does at each width</h3>
    <div class="ui-table-wrap">
      <table class="ui-table">
        <thead><tr><th class="label">Width</th><th class="label">What changes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{ARTICLE.map(([w, t]) => <tr><td class="ui-num">{w}</td><td>{t}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ui-section" id="accessibility">
    <h2 class="ui-h2">Accessibility</h2>
    <ul class="ui-list">
      <li>WCAG 2.2 AA in both themes. The contrast figures above are measured, not assumed.</li>
      <li>A 3px focus ring on everything that takes focus: ink on links, the accent inside figures.</li>
      <li>Targets are at least 44px tall on phones.</li>
      <li>Every figure has a name: a title, a figcaption or an aria-label that states it in a sentence.</li>
      <li>Interactive figures use native buttons, radios and details, so the keyboard works without extra code.</li>
      <li>Reduced motion turns every transition off site-wide.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="ui-section" id="motion">
    <h2 class="ui-h2">Motion</h2>
    <p class="ui-p">One easing and three durations, used everywhere. Each figure has one moment: it builds once as it arrives, with a beat before the part the reader is meant to notice, and nothing loops.</p>
    <div class="ui-table-wrap">
      <table class="ui-table">
        <thead><tr><th class="label">Token</th><th class="label">Value</th><th class="label">Used for</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>--pd-ease</code></td><td><code>cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)</code></td><td>Every transition: fast out, slow settle.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--pd-fast</code></td><td class="ui-num">260ms</td><td>A unit filling, a label appearing.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--pd-base</code></td><td class="ui-num">620ms</td><td>A bar or a ledger row arriving, a line dropping in, a column rising.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>--pd-slow</code></td><td class="ui-num">900ms</td><td>A segment growing, an illustration rising into place.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <ul class="ui-list">
      <li>Figures below the fold wait for the reader; figures already on screen at load show finished.</li>
      <li>A built figure shows its parts about 0.7 seconds apart. Columns rise left to right, 55ms apart, and the event lands 0.4 seconds after.</li>
      <li>Reduced motion shows every figure finished, with no transitions.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="ui-section" id="performance">
    <h2 class="ui-h2">Performance</h2>
    <ul class="ui-list">
      <li>Stylesheets are inlined into the page and scripts load with defer, so nothing blocks the first paint.</li>
      <li>Images are WebP at 1200 wide, lazy-loaded, with width and height set.</li>
      <li>Every figure ships finished in the HTML; the kit script only takes parts away to bring them back, so nothing is drawn by script above the fold.</li>
      <li>The standard is PageSpeed in the high 90s on mobile, in every category.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Styleguide>
